+The `openremote_rev5` environment is the tested release target. Its pinned
+ESP32 framework already includes tickless scheduling and Bluetooth controller
+modem sleep; no experimental SDK configuration is required.
+
+`openremote_rev5_windows` is the same firmware with a Windows-compatible
+Bluetooth HID profile (see below). It is opt-in: `pio run` still builds
+`openremote_rev5`.
 
 ## Command-line equivalents
 
@@ -35,3 +39,31 @@ pio run
 pio run --target upload
 pio device monitor
 ```
+
+## Pairing with Windows
+
+The default build presents itself to Chromecast / Android TV. Its HID report
+descriptor declares two top-level collections that share Report ID 1, which
+Android accepts but Windows does not: pairing completes, the link stays up for a
+second, then Windows reports a driver error.
+
+Build and flash the Windows variant instead:
+
+```sh
+pio run -e openremote_rev5_windows --target upload
+```
+
+What it changes, and nothing else:
+
+- One valid keyboard collection (Report ID 1) in place of the two. The report is
+  still 10 bytes, so the Bluetooth service layout is the same as before.
+- Direction keys, OK, Back, Home, volume and mute are sent as standard keyboard
+  keys (arrows, Enter, Esc, Home, Volume Up/Down, Mute) instead of Android
+  Consumer Control "menu" usages. Windows acts on those keys.
+- Play/pause, next/previous and similar media usages are still sent as Consumer
+  Control, as before.
+
+Windows caches a device's report descriptor when it pairs, so after flashing:
+remove the remote under Windows Settings > Bluetooth & devices, choose Forget
+pairing on the remote, then pair again. Otherwise Windows keeps using the old
+descriptor and the same error returns.
diff --git a/remote/platformio.ini b/remote/platformio.ini
index 8cba41f..db1a5fa 100644
--- a/remote/platformio.ini
+++ b/remote/platformio.ini
@@ -108,3 +108,16 @@ build_flags =
     -DARDUINOJSON_STRING_LENGTH_SIZE=4
     -DARDUINOJSON_SLOT_ID_SIZE=4
     -I.
+
+; Windows Bluetooth build. Identical to openremote_rev5 except for one define,
+; OPENREMOTE_BLE_WINDOWS_HID, which swaps in a HID report descriptor Windows
+; accepts and sends navigation and volume as standard keyboard keys. The default
+; environment above is untouched, so Chromecast / Android TV builds are exactly
+; what they were. See "Pairing with Windows" in PLATFORMIO.md.
+;
+;   pio run -e openremote_rev5_windows --target upload
+[env:openremote_rev5_windows]
+extends = env:openremote_rev5
+build_flags =
+    ${env:openremote_rev5.build_flags}
+    -DOPENREMOTE_BLE_WINDOWS_HID=1
